  "title": "osf verify becomes a moon-driven checkpoint runner",
  "summary": "`osf verify` no longer runs checks in-process. It now selects the moon tasks tagged for a checkpoint (hook, pre-commit, pre-push, pull-request, schedule), hands moon the affected file list over stdin, and reads moon's run report plus each task's SARIF output back to decide a result. A new `osf check <name>` command exposes each individual check (scan, scan-staged, lint-writing, lint-skill, scan-commits) so a moon task can run one at a time, and every run appends hash-chained verification events to a local journal buffer. Supporting this: a moon adapter with a run-report lock and timeout tree-kill, git environment scrubbing so an inherited GIT_DIR/GIT_INDEX_FILE cannot redirect a call, `osf hooks install` writing hook scripts outside the repo, and a `hook post-tool` event that runs the hook checkpoint on a just-written file.",
  "lenses": [
    "architecture",
    "data-flow"
  ],
